A1. You need this much wit to become a practical joker. ~ HALF
A5. Growing pains. ~ SWELLINGS
A13. A crowd disperser. ~ ELBOW
A15. This one is a highway man. ~ AVIATOR
A17. Symbol for nickel. ~ NI
A18. You'll feel sorry for these especially if they're sent with flowers. ~ REGRETS
A20. An ocean swell. ~ WAVE
A22. A spring uprising. ~ SAP
A24. To remove locks. ~ BOB
A25. Greek letter. ~ ETA
A26. A humbug. ~ BEE
A27. An old bottle-opener. ~ SOT
A29. Befuddles. (This isn't very clear.) ~ BLURS
A32. Teddy Roosevelt, greatly reduced. ~ TR
A33. Old man sunshine. ~ SOL
A35. This is an age. ~ ERA
A36. The Wet Party. ~ SPREE
A38. Star-shaped wealthy flower. ~ ASTER
A40. Natives below the Rio Grande. ~ GREASERS
A42. A strictly feminine affair. ~ AGE
A43. The kind of a man that's welcomed by the chorus. ~ MONEYED
A45. The fuel that keeps Congress going. ~ GAS
A46. Just a big brute. ~ MASTODON
A48. What tomatoes must do before they are used for dramatic criticism. ~ RIPEN
A50. He carries an Indian on his back to make a nickel. ~ BISON
A51. This'll leave you cold. ~ ICE
A53. Even, suffering from contractions. ~ EEN
A54. Royal Navy, you know how. ~ RN
A55. These folk use things. ~ USERS
A57. This is what the lion did before he lay down with the lamb. ~ ATE
A59. Oswald (abbr.). ~ OSW
A61. A lonesome prefix. ~ UNI
A62. This is a laugh. ~ AHA
A64. Miss Hepatica's first name. ~ SAL
A66. A woman supporter. ~ STAY
A68. The dumbest yet. ~ DENSEST
A71. What used cars ought to do if you believe the salesmen. ~ GO
A72. Domain. ~ DEMESNE
A74. Footpads. ~ HEELS
A76. These love 'em and leave 'em. ~ DESERTERS
A77. This is used to fill hollow legs (pl.). ~ ALES

D1. Egg plants. ~ HENS
D2. A crook's pen name. ~ ALIAS
D3. This weighs a pound. ~ LB
D4. This is in favor. ~ FOR
D6. A bird of a note. ~ WARBLE
D7. The old apple woman. ~ EVE
D8. Booksy. ~ LITERARY
D9. These surround the vamp. ~ LASTS
D10. The Clara Bowline. ~ IT
D11. The time to do it. ~ NOW
D12. Boarding-house reach. ~ GRAB
D14. A fly trap. ~ WEB
D16. The lady of the lord. ~ PEERESS
D19. This collects around port. ~ GOB
D21. Dora thinks this a horse doctor, but it's really an oldtimer. ~ VETERAN
D23. To own. ~ POSSESS
D28. This is usually up in arms. ~ TOT
D30. Need for hurry. ~ URGENCE
D31. These are never without their downs. ~ UPS
D34. This is no sweet squeeze. ~ LEMON
D36. Girl's name. ~ SADIE
D37. The high-school terrors. ~ REGENTS
D38. How to lean when with her. ~ AGAINST
D39. A Chicago flame-thrower. ~ ROD
D41. Ever, suffering from contractions. ~ EER
D42. Hiss, hiss! He's off to the Cannibal Islands. ~ AMBROSE
D44. The least peaceful or quietest. ~ NOISIEST
D47. A heavyweight. ~ TON
D49. The kind of green on the Owl and the Pussy Cat's boat. ~ PEA
D52. This is good for a rubdown. ~ ERASER
D55. A kind of dog you're always sorry for. ~ UNDER
D56. The other name for her. ~ SHE
D58. A moneyed flier. ~ EAGLE
D60. How to get water on the knee. ~ WADE
D63. An old, old flame. ~ ASH
D65. There never was any profit in this. ~ LOSS
D67. An agreement. ~ YES
D69. A compass point, high and a bit to the right. ~ NNE
D70. The scandal-time beverage. ~ TEA
D73. Nobody else. ~ ME
D75. You take this when you take the high road. ~ EL


